<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">American frontier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Old West, Wild West</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The American frontier is the history, geography and culture of the westward wave of American settlement in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, from the European colonies of the early 17th century to the admission of the last contiguous western territories as states in 1912. Encouraged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>, it carried the idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manifest-destiny">manifest destiny</a> and was spurred by laws such as the Homestead Act of 1862. It was a story of conquest as well as settlement: Indian wars were fought across the West, and the Native population fell from 600,000 in 1800 to as few as 250,000 by 1890, mostly through disease. The frontier myth became a defining part of American national identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-frontier.jpg" alt="American frontier" width="330" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Francis Barry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitting_bull_and_buffalo_bill_c1885.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The frontier is the margin of undeveloped territory beyond the established frontier line, the outer boundary of European-American settlement. The Census Bureau counted as frontier land that was generally unoccupied, with fewer than two people per square mile. The line moved steadily westward from the 1600s into the 1900s, though pockets of settlement, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> and Salt Lake City, appeared far beyond it. Historians define the American West variously, as lands west of the 98th meridian or all those west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> or Missouri rivers.</p><p>The frontier began with the English settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, in 1607. Until about 1680 it was any interior part of the continent beyond the fringe of settlements on the Atlantic coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> set up fur-trading posts in the Hudson valley, granted land to patroons who brought tenant farmers, and did not push west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>After 1783 the states gave Congress control of the western lands, and the Northwest Ordinance of 1787 abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> and promised statehood once a territory reached a threshold population, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> did in 1803. The first great movement west of the Appalachian mountains came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and North Carolina after the Revolutionary War. Pioneers lived in lean-tos or one-room log cabins, ate game, cleared patches for corn and wheat, and later added hogs, sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>.</p><p>After the Civil War the federal government subdivided territories to prepare them for statehood. The Bureau of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> of 1894 reckoned more than 40 Indian wars, costing about 19,000 white lives and about 30,000 Indian ones, and noted that the real number of Indian dead must be much higher. Historian Russell Thornton ascribes the depopulation of Native peoples mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other infectious diseases, while several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> tribes were extinguished in conflicts with Texan settlers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1890 the superintendent announced that there was no longer a clear line of advancing settlement, and so no contiguous frontier. The later census of 1900 still shows the line, and by 1910 only pockets remained, allowing travel across the continent without crossing a frontier line.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-jackson-turner">Frederick Jackson Turner</a>'s Frontier Thesis, proclaimed in 1893, shaped the main lines of scholarship for three or four generations and appeared in practically all American textbooks. The mythologising of the West began with minstrel shows and popular music in the 1840s, and the Western genre has become one of the defining features of American national identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “American frontier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
